## 2. Quantum Data Pipeline
The process of transforming classical inputs into a quantum-computable format is non-trivial and dictates the hardware requirements.

**Pipeline Flow:**
`Classical Data` $\rightarrow$ `State Preparation (Ground)` $\rightarrow$ `Encoding (Pure State)` $\rightarrow$ `Processing (Transformation)` $\rightarrow$ `Decoherence (Mixed State/Noise)` $\rightarrow$ `Measurement (Collapse to Bit String)`

### 2.1 Encoding Methodologies
The choice of encoding determines circuit depth and qubit overhead.

| Method | Scaling ($n$ qubits) | Pros | Cons |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Basis Encoding** | $1:1$ per bit | Simple mapping | No superposition; inefficient for continuous data. |
| **Angle Encoding** | $n_{qubits} = n_{features}$ | Parameterized rotation | Qubit-heavy for high-dimensional data. |
| **Amplitude Encoding** | $n_{qubits} = \log_2(n_{features})$ | Highly efficient scaling | Requires deep, complex circuits; highly noise-sensitive. |

